        "original_id": "cb85cdfa-7078-54bb-9360-fa009ef7071c",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Cox Point, Garfield Glacier, Hull Bay, Marie Byrd Land, Coor Crags, Erickson Bluffs\n- Organizations: United States Antarctic Service\n- People: Richard E. Byrd\n- Topics: Antarctic exploration\n\nFacts:\n- Richard E. Byrd was born on October 25, 1888, and died on March 11, 1957.\n- Richard E. Byrd was an American naval officer who specialized in feats of exploration.\n- The United States Antarctic Service observed and photographed Cox Point from aircraft during 1939–1941.\n- Richard E. Byrd led the United States Antarctic Service during 1939–1941.\n- Cox Point is a rock headland at the southwest side of the terminus of Garfield Glacier, where Garfield Glacier discharges into Hull Bay on the coast of Marie Byrd Land.\n- Coor Crags are several rock crags located 3.5 nautical miles southeast of Cox Point in the northern part of the Erickson Bluffs near the coast of Marie Byrd Land.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
